WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:05.529
This is a preliminary set of details about
the webinar.

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:07.110
I am Peter Cooper.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:11.210
Rana Morris will be presenting this.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.950
Any questions we get with the answers will
be available after the webinar, the link to

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:16.110
the webinars page.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.740
They will also be in the materials directory
on the ftp site.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:29.150
The shortcut link there, go.USA.gov/xnZ2B.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.590
So with that I will turn this over to Rana.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:35.339
Hello guys and thank you for joining us today.

00:00:35.339 --> 00:00:40.319
Today's talk will be about Keeping Current
and Getting Help with NCBI Resources .

00:00:40.319 --> 00:00:44.120
In the short webinar you will learn several
ways that can help you keep up-to-date with

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.100
changes and updates that are happening at
NCBI on the website and with the data.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.389
And also where to look and ways to find help
using NCBI resources .

00:00:54.389 --> 00:00:57.820
As a US government agency and part of the
national laboratory of medicine we are always

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.179
keeping in mind we are here to serve you.

00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:05.740
We have a specific mission to perform computational
data analysis research, gather data from all

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:11.390
of the globe, create systems for you to access
and use the data, and help you figure out

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:12.659
how to use it.

00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:16.990
As part of this mission we are not only putting
on this webinar today but have created various

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:23.240
mechanisms to communicate with you About the
cool things going on at NCBI and how to use

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:28.009
them . And how to provide help for you to
use them.

00:01:28.009 --> 00:01:33.350
To recap what you may already know, we have
lots and lots of databases with lots of different

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.090
kinds of data.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.579
Currently there are over 40 different databases
available on the NCBI website.

00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:44.740
We also create lots of tools to help you access
and analyze the data in the databases.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:50.280
We are updating existing and creating new
resources all the time .

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.890
Within those databases there are huge amount
of data which is constantly being added to.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:59.829
Some databases updated every day and some
updated regularly, such as GenBank.

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:02.659
With specific periodic releases.

00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:06.329
For those of you who have visited our website
over the years you may have noticed some of

00:02:06.329 --> 00:02:09.869
the pages still looks similar to how they
did when they were first released.

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:15.879
We are in the process of updating the pages
to provide an easier to read and use layout

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:16.879
of our data.

00:02:16.879 --> 00:02:22.120
This is a new initiative and you will see
some major changes coming up in the next couple

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.209
of years.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:28.440
For example for the db SNP pages which have
been around for a decade, we are now testing

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:35.540
a new design that will help dbSNP users find
what they need more easily.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:41.670
We have a Feedback button, that's the green
button on the left, we also want to hear what

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:45.819
you think about these pages, what you like
and what works well for you.

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:49.840
As well as what we might change to make it
better .

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:53.840
In addition to creating better webpages we
are also looking at what is under the hood

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.860
to improve what you see back after you do
a search.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:04.069
An example here is the new PubMed labs project,
which is testing a new search algorithm to

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:06.730
help with how literature restlts are found.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:12.760
In the process we are testing new modern displays
and adding cool new functionality.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.750
PubMed labs is a sandbox to test this out.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:21.930
The things we find here will be really good
and helpful and the ones that people like

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:29.780
and can use we will eventually add and improve
the interfaces.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.670
This is a lot of stuff.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:33.799
And a lot of stuff going on at NCBI.

00:03:33.799 --> 00:03:39.470
New data every day, updated functionality,
updated web page layouts, new resources and

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.680
cool new things coming online all the time.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.780
How can you keep up with it all?

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.930
We have a lot of channels to help you with
this.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:52.100
There are many ways to find out what is new
at NCBI and many different tutorial and help

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.719
documents to help you use the resources.

00:03:55.719 --> 00:04:02.540
We even put online recently a NCBI support
center to help you find answers to your questions,

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:08.370
to assist sending messages to the helpdesk
to get your specific questions answered.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:11.239
Don't worry about the icons now, we will go
through them all.

00:04:11.239 --> 00:04:16.220
I have a cheat sheet at the end of the presentation
which summarizes pretty succinctly everything

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.890
you need to know.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:21.380
And we'll tell you how to get a copy of this
cheat sheet yourself.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:28.770
So amongst the vastness, where is a good place
to learn what is new?

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.261
Here is the NCBI homepage.

00:04:30.261 --> 00:04:35.110
I will show you how to access a lot of the
things from a single page.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:40.200
I will recommend that you start at the NCBI
Learn site which will connect you to everything

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.530
we will talk about today pretty much on a
single page.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.610
Click on learn to get to this page.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:50.040
You may have seen this page before but if
you have not it's worthwhile.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:55.480
This is where we announce at all of the webinars
like the one you are looking at today.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:00.910
Click on the webinars and courses box and
it will take you to a list of teaching events

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:02.370
and what we are doing.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:05.450
Here is the webinar you are in today.

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:10.450
Here is where you did or you could have registered
to participate.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:14.280
While most of you probably heard about this
webinar on Twitter or Facebook, there is a

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.640
reason to come to this webinar and courses
page.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:22.820
Here is where we put links to the materials
for the webinar as soon as it is done.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:29.700
We put up the PowerPoint slides you are actually
seeing, the Q&amp;A documents answering questions

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:34.440
that we did not get to live, and for this
particular webinar I have a cheat sheet in

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.100
there that I will tell you about later on.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:43.100
Finally we will put a link to the video made
in this webinar that will be put on YouTube

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.030
in a couple of days.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:49.880
While many of you may just watch the video
on YouTube I know several of you might like

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:55.770
the PowerPoint from the webinar presentation,
to use to update yourself or to use with your

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.910
colleagues.

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:02.050
Back to the main learn page on the right-hand
side, you may notice something that looks

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:08.050
a lot like what was on the main NCBI webpage
also . The NCBI News and Blog section with

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:09.490
titles and links.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:14.750
This is the same, and we also have another
place where you can get to the NCBI insights

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:16.350
blog on the bottom.

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:22.720
Here's where we make major and minor announcements,
provide answers for frequently asked questions

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:28.310
and to basically let people know what's going
on here at NCBI and on the website .

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:32.860
For example, last week we did another one
of these NCBI minute webinars to let people

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:37.360
who write scripts and code know about a new
tool, an API key.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:41.430
We are making it available to help access
the servers.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:43.070
This is a WordPress style blog.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:51.080
so you can set up to get email alerts or RSS
feeds on the new posts that come out.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:56.710
We also created several other social media
accounts and link them here to provide this

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:59.130
information if you don't have a WordPress
account.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:03.770
Or prefer to use Twitter, Facebook or LinkedIn
instead.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:08.080
You don't have to go through the NCBI insights
blog to get to the social media accounts.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:13.610
Back on the Learn page again, there are two
or three different spots to get into the feeds,

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:17.810
channels and sites for your favorite social
media platform.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:22.910
You're welcome to sign up to follow us on
any of the social media sites.

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:30.610
Where we have showcase pages where we segregate
notifications into pertinent areas of genomes,

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:34.100
genes, chemicals, and health related subjects.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:40.020
Keep in mind everything that goes on the NCBI
blog is also announced on each of these other

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.770
social media platforms . You don't need to
worry that you will miss anything.

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:49.470
You can pick one that you are most comfortable
with and it will be able to let you know what's

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.400
going on.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:56.280
We also have a NCBI-announce email listserv
for those people who prefer to be notified

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:58.310
in their email.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:03.100
And RSS feeds.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:10.110
Just to let you know, while we often announce
our participation in various conferences on

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:12.580
all of the social media sites.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:17.830
Sometimes we will tweet more about it to let
those people who are attending know and also

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.470
want to include hashtags for the event.

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:24.880
For the most part you won't miss any of our
news or announcements regardless of whatever

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:27.200
social media site you participate in.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:29.770
You don't need to follow them all.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:36.319
Now that you found out how to be notified
of goings-on at NCBI , news and other announcements.

00:08:36.319 --> 00:08:40.419
How can you figure out how to use the cool
new things you have learned about back on

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:42.620
the NCBI Learn page.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:49.230
First there are the webinars like the one
coming up about Quick-BLASTP . You might want

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:50.899
to sign up for it.

00:08:50.899 --> 00:08:55.709
And the blog, sometimes we post showing you
how to find gene symbols and their aliases,

00:08:55.709 --> 00:08:59.389
or how to use the Genome Data Viewer, for
example.

00:08:59.389 --> 00:09:04.899
We also have written and video tutorials and
help documents to help you figure out how

00:09:04.899 --> 00:09:06.730
to do something.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:10.250
We also try to fully use the YouTube channel.

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:14.970
Not just to show the short webinars but also
we have short 1 to 3 minute videos showing

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:19.629
you how to do something very specific on our
site as well.

00:09:19.629 --> 00:09:24.000
We have a lot of different videos and even
a lot of different playlist.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.490
Many of these are actually linked directly
from our pages.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:33.259
Others are often viewed after a simple web
search.

00:09:33.259 --> 00:09:39.529
For very specific help using a particular
resource, say a database or tool, we recommend

00:09:39.529 --> 00:09:43.019
you look at the actual homepage of that resource.

00:09:43.019 --> 00:09:47.600
The developers for those resources will often
put helpful links on the left-hand side of

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:52.310
the page to assist you in learning how to
use their baby.

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:56.839
Most of the time on the website there is a
help link in the upper right hand corner of

00:09:56.839 --> 00:10:02.670
the page which will take you to the main help
documentation for that page or particular

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:03.670
resource.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:08.040
Here is the gene database homepage which has
those help links on the left.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:11.389
And some quick search terms at the bottom.

00:10:11.389 --> 00:10:16.490
It also has that main help link in the upper
right hand corner.

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:21.100
Our blast homepage also has a help link in
the upper right.

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:26.660
Both on the home page and it is still there
when you go to any particular blast page as

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:27.660
well.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:31.339
More complex tools have their own documentation.

00:10:31.339 --> 00:10:33.740
Sometimes a simple help and sometimes more.

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:38.490
Here is a picture of the variation viewer
which again in the upper right hand corner

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:44.389
has help, and also an FAQ link to answer frequently
asked questions.

00:10:44.389 --> 00:10:49.890
It also has a direct link to helpful videos
on the YouTube channel as well.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:54.980
Those links that were on the left-hand side
of the home pages, they often go to help manuals

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:58.410
and FAQ chapters on the NCBI Bookshelf.

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:03.759
There's a lot of help there that you can either
get to from the resource or you can go to

00:11:03.759 --> 00:11:06.769
the Books database and search.

00:11:06.769 --> 00:11:13.029
We really recommend the NCBI handbook , which
has chapters for each major database and tool,

00:11:13.029 --> 00:11:18.690
and describes pretty technical aspects of
the resources like history, where the data

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:23.220
that come from, access and related tools and
resources.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:27.699
For more specific and practical help on how
to use a resource, there are a lot of different

00:11:27.699 --> 00:11:30.709
help manuals designed just to do that.

00:11:30.709 --> 00:11:34.500
To show you how to use the resource practically.

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:39.279
Finally if you need specific help to answer
a question, we have a brand-new resource for

00:11:39.279 --> 00:11:40.279
you.

00:11:40.279 --> 00:11:46.579
It can be accessed from virtually any footer
at in NCBI.

00:11:46.579 --> 00:11:52.740
On the bottom right hand corner, you will
see the link to the NCBI support Center . The

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:58.959
NCBI support Center is a place where you can
find discrete answers to important questions.

00:11:58.959 --> 00:12:05.600
Some of the questions are, can I get a subscription
to access the journals in PubMed?

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:08.209
How do I cite blast in my manuscript.

00:12:08.209 --> 00:12:13.269
How can I find all the protein sequences that
belong to a certain protein family.

00:12:13.269 --> 00:12:17.439
How can I find a genome for my organism of
interest.

00:12:17.439 --> 00:12:20.529
And a whole lot of other questions as well.

00:12:20.529 --> 00:12:25.209
If you still can't quite figure out the answer
to your question, there is a link to write

00:12:25.209 --> 00:12:30.309
to the helpdesk on the top of the page, which
will create a help ticket for the member of

00:12:30.309 --> 00:12:31.700
the helpdesk to answer.

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:37.199
We will do our best to answer the question
and get back to you within two working days.

00:12:37.199 --> 00:12:40.160
Often you will get a response quicker than
that.

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:44.470
Please note that we do not answer user help
request on the social media platforms, such

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:50.839
as Facebook, as the space limitations prevent
us from being able to help as much as we need

00:12:50.839 --> 00:12:51.899
to.

00:12:51.899 --> 00:12:55.519
Also we don't have the personnel to be able
to monitor all of the channels.

00:12:55.519 --> 00:13:00.140
So we really go through directly through the
helpdesk link here.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:05.699
And yes those of you familiar with it can
still use the old NCBI helpdesk email address,

00:13:05.699 --> 00:13:10.860
which is funneled to the new system . That
email address is still info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:17.899
That is a lot of stuff and a lot of resources
and sites to remember.

